WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.737
Domain-Driven Design.

2
00:00:02.017 --> 00:00:06.091
Chapter 33, from the Architect part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:08.801
At a glance:

4
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:10.255
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:10.535 --> 00:00:11.537
Reader bands:

6
00:00:11.817 --> 00:00:14.100
B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:19.607
Ch 13, Ch 15, Ch 16, Ch 31, What you will build:

8
00:00:19.887 --> 00:00:40.580
How to draw boundaries that match the business, strategic DDD (subdomains, bounded contexts, context maps, ubiquitous language) and tactical DDD (aggregates, entities, value objects, domain events, repositories), applied to the grocery platform, with four runnable labs that measure what good and bad boundaries do.

9
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.767
L1: rich aggregate versus anemic model.

10
00:00:45.047 --> 00:00:53.581
The same 9 operations, a UI bug, bulk abuse, an admin tool, an oversized coupon, a second code path, a client retry,

11
00:00:53.861 --> 00:01:15.642
left the anemic Order invalid after 8 of 9 (ending CANCELLED with a negative quantity and a total of minus 4,999.01 rupeesagainst a real subtotal of 1,455 rupees); the aggregate stayed valid after all 9, treating the retried cancel as an idempotent no-op.

12
00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:18.717
L2: aggregate size versus contention.

13
00:01:18.997 --> 00:01:40.053
20,000 stock reservations, 16 in flight: one store-wide aggregate took 16.0x the ideal time and wasted 93.7% of work on version conflicts; per-SKU aggregates 3.2x; per-SKU with the 5 hot SKUs split into stock buckets 1.1x.

14
00:01:41.403 --> 00:01:43.429
L3: anti-corruption layer.

15
00:01:43.709 --> 00:02:06.323
12 legacy ERP rows: the naive mapping put a wrong product into the Catalog for 9 of 12 (unnormalized units, 78.85 rupees, then 78.84, rupeesa discontinued item on sale, a blank tax code treated as exempt); the ACL produced 0 wrong products, 8 translated, 4 quarantined with reasons.

16
00:02:06.603 --> 00:02:08.821
L4: finding bounded contexts.

17
00:02:09.101 --> 00:02:14.093
Splitting by nouns made 78.9% of weekly work cross boundaries.

18
00:02:14.373 --> 00:02:23.632
Modularity clustering independently found Catalog and Fulfilment contexts, and challenged the domain experts' draft in two concrete places.

19
00:02:23.912 --> 00:02:24.893
Reading time:

20
00:02:25.173 --> 00:02:27.093
130 min + code.

21
00:02:28.443 --> 00:02:29.297
Part 1.

22
00:02:29.577 --> 00:02:31.497
Introduction and Why It Matters.

23
00:02:32.747 --> 00:02:35.840
A story: twenty-six services and one ball of mud.

24
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:45.869
When the grocery platform moved to microservices, the team drew the service boundaries in an afternoon: one service per database table that felt important.

25
00:02:46.149 --> 00:02:47.237
Product service.

26
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:48.605
Price service.

27
00:02:48.885 --> 00:02:49.995
Stock service.

28
00:02:50.275 --> 00:02:51.299
Cart service.

29
00:02:51.579 --> 00:02:52.624
Order service.

30
00:02:52.904 --> 00:02:54.099
Customer service.

31
00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:55.509
Address service.

32
00:02:55.789 --> 00:02:56.920
Coupon service.

33
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.459
Twenty-six in all.

34
00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:01.832
Eighteen months later, the symptoms were unmistakable:

35
00:03:02.112 --> 00:03:04.181
Every feature touched five services.

36
00:03:04.461 --> 00:03:17.027
Adding buy 2, get 1 free required changes to Coupon, Price, Cart, Order and Product services, five pull requests across four teams, and a release train to deploy them in the right order.

37
00:03:18.377 --> 00:03:20.574
The word product meant four different things.

38
00:03:20.854 --> 00:03:34.550
To the catalog team it was a description with images; to pricing, a price book entry; to inventory, a stock-keeping unit in a specific dark store; to fulfilment, a physical item with a weight and a temperature class.

39
00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:41.294
The shared Product table had 63 columns, 40 of them nullable, and every team was afraid to change it.

40
00:03:41.574 --> 00:03:43.921
Business rules lived nowhere in particular.

41
00:03:44.201 --> 00:04:00.606
The rule an order can't be modified after it's packed was enforced in the Order service, re-implemented (differently) in the admin tool, and forgotten in the customer-support tool, which is how a support agent added items to an order already on a rider's bike (L1).

42
00:04:01.956 --> 00:04:03.983
One service became a global lock.

43
00:04:04.263 --> 00:04:14.289
Stock was modeled as a single store inventory document per dark store, so during a flash sale every reservation in the store queued behind every other (L2).

44
00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:17.279
The legacy ERP's model leaked everywhere.

45
00:04:17.559 --> 00:04:26.775
Its unit codes, status codes and rupee-as-float prices appeared in every service that touched products, each with its own conversion bugs (L3).

46
00:04:28.125 --> 00:04:31.090
The team had microservices, but not boundaries.

47
00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:39.242
They'd split the system by nouns, not by the business capabilities, language and consistency rules that actually belong together.

48
00:04:39.522 --> 00:04:47.458
The re-architecture started not with technology but with a two-day event-storming workshop and a question: where does each word mean one thing?

49
00:04:48.808 --> 00:04:49.491
Quote:

50
00:04:49.771 --> 00:04:51.435
The thesis of this chapter.

51
00:04:51.715 --> 00:04:56.045
Domain-Driven Design is a way to make software boundaries follow the business.

52
00:04:56.325 --> 00:05:09.000
Strategically, you find the places where the language and rules of the business are consistent, bounded contexts, and draw your module, service and team boundaries there, with explicit relationships (a context map) between them.

53
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:24.019
Tactically, inside a context you model the business in code: aggregates that own their invariants, value objects that make illegal values unrepresentable, and domain events that tell other contexts what happened.

54
00:05:24.299 --> 00:05:31.403
The payoff is changes that stay local, rules that can't be bypassed, and services that don't need to be deployed together.

55
00:05:32.753 --> 00:05:35.057
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

56
00:05:35.337 --> 00:05:36.681
Here is a quick table:

57
00:05:36.961 --> 00:05:39.947
DDD concept, and Grocery platform, and Lab.

58
