93
00:06:58.962 --> 00:07:00.285
Learning objectives.

94
00:07:00.565 --> 00:07:02.463
By the end you will be able to:

95
00:07:02.743 --> 00:07:07.330
Distinguish core, supporting and generic subdomains and invest accordingly.

96
00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:15.055
Find bounded contexts using ubiquitous language, invariants and usage data (L4), and draw a context map.

97
00:07:15.335 --> 00:07:25.959
Choose context-integration patterns, ACL, open host service, published language, customer or supplier, conformist, shared kernel, and build an ACL (L3).

98
00:07:27.309 --> 00:07:33.027
Design aggregates that enforce invariants (L1) and are sized for concurrency (L2).

99
00:07:33.307 --> 00:07:38.256
Use value objects, domain events, repositories and domain services correctly.

100
00:07:38.536 --> 00:07:42.397
Map bounded contexts to services and teams, and know when not to.

101
00:07:43.747 --> 00:07:44.622
Part 2.

102
00:07:44.902 --> 00:07:46.779
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

103
00:07:48.029 --> 00:07:50.952
Intuition: model the business, not the database.

104
00:07:51.232 --> 00:07:59.424
Most enterprise code starts from the database: tables become classes, classes get getters and setters, and services manipulate them.

105
00:07:59.704 --> 00:08:08.600
It works for CRUD, and falls apart when the business rules get interesting, because the rules end up scattered across every place that writes data (L1).

106
00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:17.947
DDD, introduced by Eric Evans in 2003, flips the starting point: start from how domain experts talk about and run the business.

107
00:08:18.227 --> 00:08:19.592
What are the things that matter?

108
00:08:19.872 --> 00:08:21.643
What rules must always hold?

109
00:08:21.923 --> 00:08:23.544
What happens, in what order?

110
00:08:23.824 --> 00:08:25.915
Where does the same word mean different things?

111
00:08:26.195 --> 00:08:35.411
The code should read like the business, order.place(), slot.book(order), stock.reserve(qty), and the boundaries should sit where the business itself has seams.

112
00:08:36.761 --> 00:08:39.513
DDD has two halves that are often confused:

113
00:08:39.793 --> 00:08:46.641
Strategic DDD, the big picture: subdomains, bounded contexts, context maps, ubiquitous language.

114
00:08:46.921 --> 00:08:53.150
This is where the architectural value is, and it applies whether you build a monolith or microservices.

115
00:08:53.430 --> 00:09:03.329
Tactical DDD, the patterns inside one context: aggregates, entities, value objects, domain events, repositories, domain services, factories.

116
00:09:03.609 --> 00:09:07.107
Valuable where the domain is complex; overkill for CRUD.

117
00:09:08.457 --> 00:09:19.743
A common failure is adopting the tactical patterns (lots of classes called...Aggregate and...Repository) without the strategic work, which gives you the ceremony without the boundaries.

118
00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:23.674
Subdomains: core, supporting, generic.

119
00:09:23.954 --> 00:09:28.370
A domain is the business area the software serves (grocery quick-commerce).

120
00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:35.221
It decomposes into subdomains, which are problem-space areas, they exist whether or not you write software:

121
00:09:35.501 --> 00:09:39.426
Core subdomain, what differentiates the business and wins customers.

122
00:09:39.706 --> 00:09:50.522
For the grocery platform: getting the right items to the customer within the promised slot from a dark store, slot capacity, dark-store inventory under flash-sale load, dispatch.

123
00:09:50.802 --> 00:09:55.922
This is where you put your best engineers, build custom software, and apply rich DDD.

124
00:09:57.272 --> 00:10:05.635
Supporting subdomain, necessary and business-specific, but not a differentiator: catalog management, promotions, ordering workflow.

125
00:10:05.915 --> 00:10:08.325
Build it, but more simply; don't gold-plate.

126
00:10:08.605 --> 00:10:16.584
Generic subdomain, solved problems every business has: payments, SMS, identity, GST invoice formatting, email.

127
00:10:16.864 --> 00:10:27.168
Buy or use off-the-shelf (Razorpay, a notification provider, an identity provider), and integrate through an anti-corruption layer or a conformist relationship.

128
00:10:28.518 --> 00:10:45.627
The classification is an investment decision (Ch 31): the §1.1 platform spent months building its own coupon engine (supporting) and its own OTP delivery (generic) while its slot allocation (core) was a spreadsheet-driven batch job.

129
00:10:46.977 --> 00:10:48.321
Ubiquitous language.

130
00:10:48.601 --> 00:11:01.081
Ubiquitous language is a shared vocabulary between domain experts and developers, used everywhere, in conversations, documents, and the code itself (class names, method names, event names).

131
00:11:01.361 --> 00:11:10.833
If the dark-store manager says we short-pick an item when the shelf is empty, the code has a ShortPick event, not ItemStatusUpdate(status=4).

132
00:11:11.113 --> 00:11:12.159
Why it matters:

133
00:11:12.439 --> 00:11:14.423
Translation is where bugs hide.

134
00:11:14.703 --> 00:11:19.780
Every time a developer maps what the business said to what the code calls it, meaning is lost.

135
00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:22.666
It exposes boundaries.

136
00:11:22.946 --> 00:11:34.210
When the same word means different things to different groups, product, order, customer, address, that's a signal of different bounded contexts, each with its own model (§2.4).

137
00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:45.221
It makes code reviewable by the business. if (order.isPacked()) throw new OrderAlreadyPacked() can be checked with an operations manager; if (o.st = 4) cannot.

138
00:11:45.501 --> 00:11:50.493
The language is bounded: it's ubiquitous within a context, not across the enterprise.

139
00:11:50.773 --> 00:12:00.181
Trying to create one enterprise-wide canonical model with one definition of Product is how you get §1.1's 63-column table.

140
00:12:01.531 --> 00:12:02.768
Bounded contexts.

141
00:12:03.048 --> 00:12:08.680
A bounded context is a boundary within which a particular model and its language are consistent.

142
00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:14.592
Inside it, Product means exactly one thing; outside, it may mean something else entirely.

143
00:12:15.942 --> 00:12:17.286
Here is a quick table:

144
00:12:17.566 --> 00:12:20.318
Context, and What Product means there, and Model.

145
00:12:20.598 --> 00:12:21.451
Catalog.

146
00:12:21.731 --> 00:12:23.139
What Product means there:

147
00:12:23.419 --> 00:12:27.195
A listing: name, description, images, category, attributes.

148
00:12:27.475 --> 00:12:28.222
Model:

149
00:12:28.502 --> 00:12:29.569
ProductListing.

150
00:12:29.849 --> 00:12:30.681
Pricing.

151
00:12:30.961 --> 00:12:32.369
What Product means there:

152
00:12:32.649 --> 00:12:33.886
A price book entry:

153
00:12:34.166 --> 00:12:36.683
MRP, selling price, effective dates.

154
00:12:36.963 --> 00:12:37.710
Model:

155
00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:39.163
PriceBookEntry.

156
00:12:39.443 --> 00:12:40.382
Inventory.

157
00:12:40.662 --> 00:12:42.070
What Product means there:

158
00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:46.275
Stock of one SKU in one dark store: on-hand, reserved.

159
00:12:46.555 --> 00:12:47.302
Model:

160
00:12:47.582 --> 00:12:48.563
SkuStock.

161
00:12:48.843 --> 00:12:49.825
Fulfilment.

162
00:12:50.105 --> 00:12:51.513
What Product means there:

163
00:12:51.793 --> 00:12:55.462
A physical item: weight, temperature class, bin location.

164
00:12:55.742 --> 00:12:56.489
Model:

165
00:12:56.769 --> 00:12:57.750
PickItem.

166
00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:02.769
They share an identifier (the SKU code) but not a model.

167
00:13:03.049 --> 00:13:05.865
Each context evolves its model independently.

168
00:13:06.145 --> 00:13:09.409
How to find bounded contexts (combine several signals):

169
00:13:09.689 --> 00:13:12.633
Language, where does the meaning of a key term change?

170
00:13:12.913 --> 00:13:16.711
Invariants, which data must be consistent together, immediately?

171
00:13:16.991 --> 00:13:20.895
That data belongs in one context (and usually one aggregate).

172
00:13:21.175 --> 00:13:25.996
Business capabilities and processes, fulfil an order versus manage the catalog.

173
00:13:27.346 --> 00:13:30.205
Change patterns, what changes together in practice?

174
00:13:30.485 --> 00:13:33.407
(Change coupling from git history is good evidence.).

175
00:13:33.687 --> 00:13:37.293
Usage patterns, which entities do use cases touch together?

176
00:13:37.573 --> 00:13:40.133
(L4 builds this from event-storming output.).

177
00:13:40.413 --> 00:13:49.287
Team ownership, Conway's Law (Ch 37): a context owned by one team stays coherent; one owned by three teams fractures.

178
00:13:50.637 --> 00:14:10.563
L4 shows that usage data is a useful challenge to a human draft but not a substitute for it: modularity clustering independently found the Catalog and Fulfilment contexts, and pulled Address into Fulfilment (the busy use cases need a delivery location, not the customer's address book, a real modeling insight).

179
00:14:10.843 --> 00:14:18.608
It also proposed a grouping, Cart, Coupon, StockLevel, that follows call patterns rather than any invariant or language.

180
00:14:18.888 --> 00:14:21.448
Bounded context is not microservice.

181
00:14:21.728 --> 00:14:23.776
A context is a model boundary.

182
00:14:24.056 --> 00:14:30.115
It can be one service, several services (if it's big), or a module inside a modular monolith.

183
00:14:30.395 --> 00:14:35.387
What it should almost never be is split across teams or shared by several contexts' code.

184
00:14:36.737 --> 00:14:39.062
Context maps and integration patterns.

185
00:14:39.342 --> 00:14:49.603
A context map shows the bounded contexts and the relationships between them, both technical (how they integrate) and organizational (who has power over whose model).

186
00:14:49.883 --> 00:14:51.825
The standard relationship patterns:

187
00:14:53.175 --> 00:14:54.519
Here is a quick table:

188
00:14:54.799 --> 00:14:57.401
Pattern, and Meaning, and Grocery example.

189
00:14:57.681 --> 00:14:58.599
Partnership.

190
00:14:58.879 --> 00:14:59.668
Meaning:

191
00:14:59.948 --> 00:15:03.873
Two teams succeed or fail together; coordinate changes closely.

192
00:15:04.153 --> 00:15:05.391
Grocery example:

193
00:15:05.671 --> 00:15:08.892
Ordering + Fulfilment during the slot-booking rebuild.

194
00:15:09.172 --> 00:15:10.153
Shared kernel.

195
00:15:10.433 --> 00:15:11.223
Meaning:

196
00:15:11.503 --> 00:15:15.343
Two contexts share a small, jointly-owned piece of model or code.

197
00:15:15.623 --> 00:15:16.860
Grocery example:

198
00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:20.660
A shared Money or Sku value-object library, keep it tiny.

199
00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.369
Customer or Supplier.

200
00:15:22.649 --> 00:15:23.439
Meaning:

201
00:15:23.719 --> 00:15:28.220
Downstream (customer) needs influence upstream (supplier) priorities.

202
00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:29.737
Grocery example:

203
00:15:30.017 --> 00:15:33.153
Ordering (customer) ← Inventory (supplier).

204
00:15:33.433 --> 00:15:34.372
Conformist.

205
00:15:34.652 --> 00:15:35.441
Meaning:

206
00:15:35.721 --> 00:15:39.412
Downstream adopts upstream's model as-is, with no influence.

207
00:15:39.692 --> 00:15:40.929
Grocery example:

208
00:15:41.209 --> 00:15:43.897
Billing conforms to Razorpay's payment model.

209
00:15:44.177 --> 00:15:46.289
Anti-corruption layer (ACL).

210
00:15:46.569 --> 00:15:47.359
Meaning:

211
00:15:47.639 --> 00:15:51.479
Downstream translates upstream's model into its own, protecting it.

212
00:15:51.759 --> 00:15:52.996
Grocery example:

213
00:15:53.276 --> 00:15:56.391
Catalog ← legacy ERP (L3).

214
00:15:57.741 --> 00:15:59.789
Open host service (OHS).

215
00:16:00.069 --> 00:16:00.858
Meaning:

216
00:16:01.138 --> 00:16:05.895
Upstream offers a well-defined protocol or API for many consumers.

217
00:16:06.175 --> 00:16:07.413
Grocery example:

218
00:16:07.693 --> 00:16:09.954
Catalog's public product API.

219
00:16:10.234 --> 00:16:11.450
Published language.

220
00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:12.519
Meaning:

221
00:16:12.799 --> 00:16:14.890
A documented shared exchange format.

222
00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.407
Grocery example:

223
00:16:16.687 --> 00:16:20.933
Versioned OrderPlaced event schema in the registry (Ch 28).

224
00:16:21.213 --> 00:16:22.301
Separate ways.

225
00:16:22.581 --> 00:16:23.370
Meaning:

226
00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:25.869
No integration; duplicate instead.

227
00:16:26.149 --> 00:16:27.386
Grocery example:

228
00:16:27.666 --> 00:16:30.226
Marketing's own product copy for campaigns.

229
00:16:31.576 --> 00:16:38.104
The anti-corruption layer deserves emphasis because it's the most common pattern in brownfield systems (L3).

230
00:16:38.384 --> 00:16:51.547
The legacy ERP's concepts, GM or GMS or LTR unit codes, status S1..S9, rupees as floats, sometimes-blank tax codes, must not leak into the Catalog's model.

231
00:16:51.827 --> 00:17:01.939
The ACL translates into the Catalog's language (Unit, Money, Availability), uses exact arithmetic, and quarantines what it can't translate faithfully.

232
00:17:02.219 --> 00:17:08.981
In L3 the naive mapping put a wrong product into the domain for 9 of 12 rows; the ACL for none.

233
00:17:10.331 --> 00:17:12.102
Entities and value objects.

234
00:17:12.382 --> 00:17:19.209
An entity has an identity that persists through change: an Order is the same order whether it's draft or delivered.

235
00:17:19.489 --> 00:17:20.961
Equality is by ID.

236
00:17:21.241 --> 00:17:27.214
A value object is defined entirely by its attributes, is immutable, and has no identity:

237
00:17:27.494 --> 00:17:39.227
Money(2850 paise, INR), Quantity(500, ML), Pincode(560034), DeliveryLocation(lat, lng, landmark).

238
00:17:39.507 --> 00:17:43.049
Two Money(2850) are interchangeable.

239
00:17:43.329 --> 00:17:44.929
Equality is by value.

240
00:17:45.209 --> 00:17:49.582
Value objects are the most underused, highest-return tactical pattern:

241
00:17:50.932 --> 00:17:53.407
They make illegal values unrepresentable.

242
00:17:53.687 --> 00:17:59.959
A Pincode constructor that validates six digits means no code anywhere can hold an invalid pincode.

243
00:18:00.239 --> 00:18:10.436
A Money that stores paise as a long means no floating-point rounding bugs (L3's 78.85 rupees, then 78.84 rupees).

244
00:18:10.716 --> 00:18:18.140
They carry behavior. money.add(other) checks currency; quantity.convertTo(Unit.G); slotWindow.overlaps(other).

245
00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:20.959
They're trivially thread-safe and easy to test.

246
00:18:21.239 --> 00:18:24.439
In Java, record types are ideal value objects:

247
00:18:25.789 --> 00:18:29.458
Here is the Java code, Value objects as Java records.

248
00:18:32.308 --> 00:18:34.505
Aggregates: consistency boundaries.

249
00:18:34.785 --> 00:18:46.881
An aggregate is a cluster of entities and value objects treated as one unit for data changes, with one entity as the aggregate root, the only object outside code may hold a reference to.

250
00:18:47.161 --> 00:18:53.561
The aggregate's job is to protect its invariants: rules that must be true after every transaction.

251
00:18:53.841 --> 00:19:11.079
For the Order aggregate (root Order, containing OrderLine entities and Money or Quantity values): total = Σ(line qty times unit price) minus discount, derived, never stored independently;. quantities are positive and at most 10 per SKU;.

252
00:19:12.429 --> 00:19:22.711
discount ∈ 0, subtotal;. items can only be added while DRAFT;. only a PLACED order can be cancelled (and cancelling a CANCELLED order is an idempotent no-op).

253
00:19:22.991 --> 00:19:26.511
The rules of aggregate design (Vaughn Vernon's formulation):

254
00:19:26.791 --> 00:19:29.949
Protect true invariants inside the aggregate boundary.

255
00:19:30.229 --> 00:19:38.549
All changes go through the root's intention-revealing methods (addItem, applyCoupon, place), which refuse invalid transitions.

256
00:19:38.829 --> 00:19:40.130
No public setters.

257
00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:46.575
L1: the anemic Order became invalid after 8 of 9 operations; the aggregate after none.

258
00:19:47.925 --> 00:19:49.504
Design small aggregates.

259
00:19:49.784 --> 00:19:56.120
An aggregate is loaded, locked and saved as a unit, so it is also the unit of concurrency (L2).

260
00:19:56.400 --> 00:20:06.341
A store-wide StoreInventory aggregate serializes every reservation, 16x slower than ideal with 94% of work wasted on conflicts.

261
00:20:06.621 --> 00:20:20.893
A per-SKU SkuStock aggregate matches the actual invariant (stock ≥ 0 per SKU) and runs 5x faster; splitting the hottest SKUs' stock into buckets gets within 10% of ideal.

262
00:20:21.173 --> 00:20:23.605
Reference other aggregates by identity.

263
00:20:23.885 --> 00:20:27.427
An Order holds a CustomerId, not a Customer object.

264
00:20:27.707 --> 00:20:32.016
This keeps aggregates small and makes their transactional boundaries explicit.

265
00:20:33.366 --> 00:20:36.310
Update other aggregates with eventual consistency.

266
00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:39.406
One transaction modifies one aggregate.

267
00:20:39.686 --> 00:20:49.649
If placing an order must reserve stock, the Order aggregate emits OrderPlaced and the Inventory context reacts, or a saga coordinates (Ch 13).

268
00:20:49.929 --> 00:21:02.110
Rules spanning aggregates are therefore eventually consistent; if the business truly needs them to be immediately consistent, that's a sign they belong in one aggregate (and you pay the concurrency cost).

269
00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:07.769
Repositories, factories, domain services, application services.

270
00:21:08.049 --> 00:21:17.159
Repository, a collection-like interface for loading and saving aggregates (not tables): orders.byId(id), orders.save(order).

271
00:21:17.439 --> 00:21:19.828
One repository per aggregate root.

272
00:21:20.108 --> 00:21:30.519
The domain defines the interface; infrastructure implements it (JPA, JDBC), the dependency direction L2 of Ch 31 enforces.

273
00:21:30.799 --> 00:21:35.492
Factory, encapsulates complex creation so an aggregate is born valid:

274
00:21:35.772 --> 00:21:39.292
Order.startFor(customerId, cart, pricingSnapshot).

275
00:21:40.642 --> 00:21:49.175
Domain service, domain logic that doesn't naturally belong to one entity, typically involving several aggregates or an external policy:

276
00:21:49.455 --> 00:21:53.103
SlotAllocationService.bestSlotFor(order, darkStore).

277
00:21:53.383 --> 00:21:55.794
Stateless, named in the ubiquitous language.

278
00:21:56.074 --> 00:22:07.338
Application service, orchestrates a use case: load aggregates via repositories, call domain methods, save, publish events, handle transactions and authorization.

279
00:22:07.618 --> 00:22:09.431
It contains no business rules.

280
00:22:09.711 --> 00:22:15.663
If an application service has if statements about business policy, the logic is leaking out of the domain.

281
00:22:17.013 --> 00:22:21.856
Here is the Java code, Application service: orchestration only (VERIFY:

282
00:22:22.136 --> 00:22:23.267
Spring wiring).

283
00:22:23.547 --> 00:22:27.067
It runs to 15 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

284
00:22:29.917 --> 00:22:30.962
Domain events.

285
00:22:31.242 --> 00:22:39.903
A domain event records something that happened in the domain that domain experts care about, named in the past tense in the ubiquitous language:

286
00:22:40.183 --> 00:22:44.983
OrderPlaced, StockReserved, SlotBooked, ItemShortPicked, OrderDispatched.

287
00:22:45.263 --> 00:22:49.935
Within a context, events decouple side effects from the aggregate that caused them.

288
00:22:51.285 --> 00:22:55.360
Across contexts, events are the primary integration mechanism:

289
00:22:55.640 --> 00:23:00.205
Ordering publishes OrderPlaced; Inventory, Fulfilment and Billing react.

290
00:23:00.485 --> 00:23:08.571
This implements one aggregate per transaction, eventual consistency between aggregates (§2.7 rule 4).

291
00:23:08.851 --> 00:23:21.139
Reliably: save the event in the same transaction as the aggregate (the transactional outbox, Ch 15) and publish it asynchronously, otherwise you can commit the order and lose the event.

292
00:23:22.489 --> 00:23:36.761
Internal versus integration events: events inside a context can carry rich domain objects; events crossing contexts form a published language, versioned, schema-registered, minimal, and stable (Ch 28).

293
00:23:37.041 --> 00:23:55.579
Event storming (Alberto Brandolini) is the workshop technique that discovers events, commands, aggregates, policies and boundaries together with domain experts: everyone writes domain events on orange stickies along a timeline, then adds commands, actors, aggregates, policies and hotspots.

294
00:23:55.859 --> 00:23:59.699
Boundaries appear where the language changes and where flows hand off.

295
00:23:59.979 --> 00:24:05.761
L4's use-case or entity data is the kind of output an event-storming session produces.

296
00:24:07.111 --> 00:24:09.948
From bounded contexts to services and teams.

297
00:24:10.228 --> 00:24:17.161
Bounded contexts are the best starting point for service boundaries (Ch 16), but the mapping isn't mechanical:

298
00:24:17.441 --> 00:24:25.591
Start with a modular monolith where contexts are modules with enforced boundaries (fitness functions, Ch 31 L2).

299
00:24:25.871 --> 00:24:36.068
Extract a context into a service when it has a real driver: independent scaling, independent release cadence, a different team, a different availability requirement.

300
00:24:36.348 --> 00:24:40.721
One context, then one team (a team may own several small contexts).

301
00:24:41.001 --> 00:24:44.841
Never split one context across teams, its model will fracture.

302
00:24:46.191 --> 00:24:57.946
Don't split a context by technical layer (a product-read-service and a product-write-service owned by different teams) unless you're deliberately applying CQRS inside one team's context.

303
00:24:58.226 --> 00:25:07.634
Microservices smaller than a context are fine when there's a reason (a scaling hotspot), but they share the context's language and ideally its team.

304
00:25:07.914 --> 00:25:10.069
Data ownership follows the context.

305
00:25:10.349 --> 00:25:23.810
Each context owns its data; others get it through APIs, events or read models, never by querying its tables (the §1.1 shared 63-column Product table is the anti-pattern).

306
00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:26.803
When DDD is overkill.

307
00:25:27.083 --> 00:25:35.872
DDD's tactical patterns carry real cost: more classes, more mapping, eventual consistency between aggregates, and a learning curve.

308
00:25:36.152 --> 00:25:40.973
They pay off when the domain has complex, evolving rules and concurrent actors.

309
00:25:41.253 --> 00:25:42.533
They don't pay off for:

310
00:25:42.813 --> 00:25:46.120
CRUD, a settings screen, a reference-data admin page.

311
00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:50.475
Use simple data-centric code (transaction script, active record).

312
00:25:50.755 --> 00:25:54.829
Generic subdomains, you'll integrate a product, not model the domain.

313
00:25:55.109 --> 00:25:59.397
Reporting or analytics, read models and SQL, not aggregates.

314
00:26:00.747 --> 00:26:14.849
Strategic DDD, finding contexts, speaking the business's language, drawing a context map, protecting boundaries with ACLs, is almost always worth it, even in a system that uses no tactical patterns at all.

