00:36:41.157 --> 00:36:42.095
Requirement:

436
00:36:42.375 --> 00:36:46.215
During flash sales, reservations time out on version conflicts.

437
00:36:46.495 --> 00:36:48.863
Is it load, or our aggregate boundary?

438
00:36:49.143 --> 00:37:00.493
20,000 stock reservations over 300 SKUs with realistic skew (the top 5 SKUs take 43% of orders), 16 transactions in flight.

439
00:37:00.773 --> 00:37:09.178
Per round, one transaction per aggregate commits and the rest conflict and retry, exactly what an optimistic @Version check does.

440
00:37:10.528 --> 00:37:13.621
Here is the Java code, ContentionL2.java.

441
00:37:13.901 --> 00:37:17.891
It runs to 110 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

442
00:37:18.171 --> 00:37:25.723
ContentionL2.java, run with: java ContentionL2.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

443
00:37:26.003 --> 00:37:28.179
AGGREGATE SIZE versus CONTENTION.

444
00:37:28.459 --> 00:37:39.381
An aggregate is the unit of consistency AND the unit of concurrency: each transaction. loads one aggregate, changes it, and saves it with an optimistic version check.

445
00:37:39.661 --> 00:37:41.901
Two concurrent transactions on the SAME.

446
00:37:44.751 --> 00:37:45.669
On screen:

447
00:37:45.949 --> 00:38:02.845
20,000 stock reservations over 300 SKUs (Zipf s=1.1: top 5 SKUs = 43% of orders), 16. transactions in flight. aggregate design rounds conflicts wasted work versus ideal.

448
00:38:03.125 --> 00:38:13.173
A) one StoreInventory aggregate 20,000 299,880 93.7% 16.0x.

449
00:38:13.453 --> 00:38:21.666
B) one aggregate per SKU 4,007 44,012 68.8% 3.2x.

450
00:38:21.946 --> 00:38:33.594
C) per SKU + hot SKUs split into 8 stock buckets 1,382 2,102 9.5% 1.1x.

451
00:38:33.874 --> 00:38:34.706
(ideal:

452
00:38:34.986 --> 00:38:54.101
16 commits every round) 1,250. then A serializes everything: the store-wide aggregate lets exactly ONE reservation. commit per round, so throughput. collapses to 1 or 16 of capacity and almost all concurrent work is thrown away as. version conflicts.

453
00:38:54.381 --> 00:38:55.298
There is no.

454
00:38:55.578 --> 00:38:57.093
The rest is in the chapter.

455
00:38:59.943 --> 00:39:00.796
Reading it:

456
00:39:01.076 --> 00:39:05.812
A) One StoreInventory aggregate commits exactly one reservation per round:

457
00:39:06.092 --> 00:39:11.873
16x the ideal time, 93.7% of work thrown away as conflicts.

458
00:39:12.153 --> 00:39:15.865
No invariant spans SKUs, so the boundary buys nothing.

459
00:39:16.145 --> 00:39:30.247
B) Per-SKU aggregates match the real invariant (stock ≥ 0 per SKU) and are 5x faster, but still 3.2x ideal, because milk and bread (the Zipf head) keep colliding.

460
00:39:31.597 --> 00:39:42.370
C) Hot-SKU stock buckets split each of the 5 hottest SKUs' stock into 8 sub-aggregates, each enforcing stock ≥ 0 on its share:

461
00:39:42.650 --> 00:39:46.639
1.1x ideal, 9.5% conflicts.

462
00:39:46.919 --> 00:39:52.189
The price is allocation logic, rebalancing stock between buckets when one runs dry.

463
00:39:52.469 --> 00:39:57.311
The general rule: draw the aggregate around the invariant, then check the contention.

464
00:39:57.591 --> 00:40:03.159
Conflict rate per aggregate type is the production metric that tells you a boundary is wrong.

465
00:40:04.509 --> 00:40:08.051
