506
00:44:41.068 --> 00:44:41.921
Reading it:

507
00:44:42.201 --> 00:45:02.788
The naive mapper loads every row, and puts a wrong product into the domain for 9 of 12: unit codes like gm, gms, ltr, nos leak in (a pack-size filter on g misses them), (long)(78.85 100) truncates to 78.84, rupeesa discontinued Pepsi is sellable,

508
00:45:03.068 --> 00:45:16.103
and four rows that should never go live (a 0.29 rupeesprice entry error, a blank HSN tax code, an unknown unit RL, an unknown status S9) are admitted as sellable.

509
00:45:17.453 --> 00:45:32.301
The ACL translates into Unit, Money (exact BigDecimal, then paise) and Availability; keeps the discontinued item but marks it not sellable; and quarantines 4 rows with reasons a catalog-ops person can act on.

510
00:45:32.581 --> 00:45:34.629
0 wrong products reach the domain.

511
00:45:34.909 --> 00:45:44.466
The ERP's model, its codes, its float prices, its status numbering, now exists in exactly one place: the translation tables at the boundary.

512
00:45:45.816 --> 00:45:48.611
L4, Finding bounded contexts from usage.

513
00:45:48.891 --> 00:45:49.829
Requirement:

514
00:45:50.109 --> 00:45:53.779
We split by nouns and every feature touches five services.

515
00:45:54.059 --> 00:45:55.509
Where should the boundaries be?

516
00:45:55.789 --> 00:46:04.195
Seventeen use cases from an event-storming session, each with the entities it touches and its weekly volume, form an entity-affinity graph.

517
00:46:04.475 --> 00:46:13.691
Candidate decompositions are scored by the share of weekly work that crosses contexts, the size of the largest context, and graph modularity.

518
00:46:15.041 --> 00:46:18.134
Here is the Java code, ContextsL4.java.

519
00:46:18.414 --> 00:46:22.830
It runs to 197 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

520
00:46:23.110 --> 00:46:30.726
ContextsL4.java, run with: java ContextsL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

521
00:46:31.006 --> 00:46:34.142
FINDING BOUNDED CONTEXTS FROM HOW THE DOMAIN IS USED.

522
00:46:34.422 --> 00:46:42.998
The classic mistake is to split a system by NOUNS, one service per. entity, which puts every real use case across several services.

523
00:46:43.278 --> 00:46:46.350
Bounded contexts should instead group what CHANGES and.

524
00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:50.117
On screen:

525
00:46:50.397 --> 00:46:55.005
17 use cases over 14 entities (weekly volume in thousands).

526
00:46:55.285 --> 00:47:02.901
Candidate decompositions: decomposition contexts cross-ctx % ctx per call largest modularity.

527
00:47:03.181 --> 00:47:10.819
A) one service per entity (nouns) 14 78.9% 2.16 1 to 0.11.

528
00:47:11.099 --> 00:47:18.416
B) one big module 1 0.0% 1.00 14 0.00.

529
00:47:18.696 --> 00:47:29.491
C) threshold clustering, edges ≥ 80k or wk 2 2.9% 1.03 13 0.00.

530
00:47:29.771 --> 00:47:40.864
C) threshold clustering, edges ≥ 100k or wk 9 25.8% 1.38 4 0.27.

531
00:47:41.144 --> 00:47:48.973
E) modularity clustering (Louvain) 5 36.6% 1.37 4 0.32.

532
00:47:49.253 --> 00:47:57.403
D) domain-expert draft (4 contexts) 4 29.5% 1.44 6 0.24.

533
00:47:57.683 --> 00:48:02.077
C) threshold clustering at 80k or wk (single linkage):

534
00:48:02.357 --> 00:48:06.091
Address, Cart, Customer, DeliverySlot, Invoice, Order, Payment.

535
00:48:06.371 --> 00:48:10.253
ProductDescription, ProductPrice, Reservation, Rider, Shipment.

536
00:48:10.533 --> 00:48:12.048
The rest is in the chapter.

537
00:48:14.898 --> 00:48:15.751
Reading it:

538
00:48:16.031 --> 00:48:26.143
Nouns (A) give 14 services and make 78.9% of weekly work cross boundaries, the §1.1 distributed monolith, quantified.

539
00:48:26.423 --> 00:48:40.290
Minimizing cross-context calls alone is a trap: it rewards one big module (B), and single-linkage clustering (C at 80k) chains 13 of 14 entities into one blob through a few busy use cases.

540
00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:44.861
Modularity (E) is the most informative signal.

541
00:48:45.141 --> 00:49:04.960
It independently recovers Catalog ProductDescription, ProductPrice and a Fulfilment context, and pulls Address into Fulfilment, which is a genuine modeling insight: the busy use cases need a delivery location, not the customer's address book, so model a DeliveryLocation value object copied onto the order.

542
00:49:06.310 --> 00:49:13.713
But E also proposes Cart, Coupon, StockLevel, a call-pattern artifact with no shared invariant or language.

543
00:49:13.993 --> 00:49:26.515
The domain-expert draft (D) has a lower modularity score and is still the better starting point; the data challenges it in two specific places (Address per context; where Reservation belongs).

544
00:49:26.795 --> 00:49:31.467
Algorithms provide evidence; language, invariants and ownership decide.

545
00:49:32.817 --> 00:49:33.756
Part 6.

546
00:49:34.036 --> 00:49:35.316
Production Concerns.

547
00:49:36.566 --> 00:49:37.718
Failure modes.

548
00:49:37.998 --> 00:49:39.342
Here is a quick table:

549
00:49:39.622 --> 00:49:41.905
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

550
00:49:42.185 --> 00:49:43.849
Entity services (nouns).

551
00:49:44.129 --> 00:49:44.939
Symptom:

552
00:49:45.219 --> 00:49:48.910
Every feature touches 5 services; distributed monolith.

553
00:49:49.190 --> 00:49:50.193
Mitigation:

554
00:49:50.473 --> 00:49:54.206
Bounded contexts from language, invariants, usage (L4).

555
00:49:54.486 --> 00:49:55.873
Anemic domain model.

556
00:49:56.153 --> 00:49:56.963
Symptom:

557
00:49:57.243 --> 00:50:00.337
Rules duplicated and bypassed by new code paths.

558
00:50:00.617 --> 00:50:01.619
Mitigation:

559
00:50:01.899 --> 00:50:04.886
Aggregates own invariants; no setters (L1).

560
00:50:05.166 --> 00:50:06.595
Oversized aggregate.

561
00:50:06.875 --> 00:50:07.686
Symptom:

562
00:50:07.966 --> 00:50:11.507
Version conflicts, serialized writes, timeouts under load.

563
00:50:11.787 --> 00:50:12.790
Mitigation:

564
00:50:13.070 --> 00:50:16.313
Size to the true invariant; split hot spots (L2).

565
00:50:16.593 --> 00:50:18.043
Shared canonical model.

566
00:50:18.323 --> 00:50:19.134
Symptom:

567
00:50:19.414 --> 00:50:22.379
63-column table nobody dares change.

568
00:50:22.659 --> 00:50:23.662
Mitigation:

569
00:50:23.942 --> 00:50:26.737
Per-context models; share IDs, not models.

570
00:50:27.017 --> 00:50:28.467
Leaking upstream model.

571
00:50:28.747 --> 00:50:29.558
Symptom:

572
00:50:29.838 --> 00:50:32.569
Legacy codes and float money in every service.

573
00:50:32.849 --> 00:50:33.851
Mitigation:

574
00:50:34.131 --> 00:50:37.161
ACL with translation + quarantine (L3).

575
00:50:38.511 --> 00:50:40.196
Lost cross-context events.

576
00:50:40.476 --> 00:50:41.287
Symptom:

577
00:50:41.567 --> 00:50:43.743
Order placed, stock never reserved.

578
00:50:44.023 --> 00:50:45.025
Mitigation:

579
00:50:45.305 --> 00:50:49.615
Transactional outbox + idempotent consumers (Ch 15).

580
00:50:49.895 --> 00:50:51.559
Cross-aggregate transaction.

581
00:50:51.839 --> 00:50:52.649
Symptom:

582
00:50:52.929 --> 00:50:55.916
Deadlocks, long locks, distributed transactions.

583
00:50:56.196 --> 00:50:57.199
Mitigation:

584
00:50:57.479 --> 00:51:01.937
One aggregate per transaction; sagas for workflows (Ch 13).

585
00:51:02.217 --> 00:51:03.945
Context split across teams.

586
00:51:04.225 --> 00:51:05.036
Symptom:

587
00:51:05.316 --> 00:51:07.705
Model fractures, conflicting changes.

588
00:51:07.985 --> 00:51:08.988
Mitigation:

589
00:51:09.268 --> 00:51:10.761
One team per context.

590
00:51:12.111 --> 00:51:13.199
Observability.

591
00:51:13.479 --> 00:51:19.645
Per-context dashboards: each bounded context has its own SLOs and owner (Ch 24).

592
00:51:19.925 --> 00:51:32.725
Domain events as telemetry: event rates (OrderPlaced per minute, ItemShortPicked per order) are business metrics; lag between OrderPlaced and StockReserved measures eventual-consistency delay.

593
00:51:33.005 --> 00:51:40.791
Aggregate conflict rate (optimistic lock failures per aggregate type) is the early warning for L2's contention problem.

594
00:51:41.071 --> 00:51:46.682
ACL quarantine volume and reasons show upstream data quality and translation gaps.

595
00:51:48.032 --> 00:51:54.432
Change coupling: the share of PRs or features touching more than one context is the metric for R1.

596
00:51:55.782 --> 00:51:56.721
Security.

597
00:51:57.001 --> 00:52:07.710
Bounded contexts are natural authorization boundaries: each context enforces access to its own aggregates; don't let another context's service account write your data.

598
00:52:07.990 --> 00:52:17.995
Value objects validate at the boundary (pincode, GSTIN, phone numbers), input validation becomes structural rather than remembered (Ch 23).

599
00:52:18.275 --> 00:52:20.323
PII lives in few contexts.

600
00:52:20.603 --> 00:52:32.337
Pass IDs, not personal data, in cross-context events; copy only what the downstream context needs (Fulfilment needs a DeliveryLocation, not the customer's full profile).

601
00:52:33.687 --> 00:52:35.052
Performance and tuning.

602
00:52:35.332 --> 00:52:47.577
Aggregate size drives load cost and lock contention: keep collections inside aggregates bounded (an Order has at most 100 lines; a Customer does not contain all their orders).

603
00:52:47.857 --> 00:52:55.281
Use optimistic locking (@Version) per aggregate; retry on conflict with backoff; monitor conflict rates (L2).

604
00:52:55.561 --> 00:53:06.377
For read-heavy views spanning contexts, build read models or CQRS projections from events rather than joining across contexts at query time (Ch 15).

605
00:53:07.727 --> 00:53:14.490
Hot aggregates: split (buckets), queue per aggregate, or accept a different consistency model for that data.

606
00:53:15.840 --> 00:53:16.565
Cost.

607
00:53:16.845 --> 00:53:22.477
DDD's cost is modeling effort and more code (value objects, translations, events).

608
00:53:22.757 --> 00:53:30.864
Concentrate it in the core subdomain; use simple code or bought products elsewhere (§2.2, §2.11).

609
00:53:31.144 --> 00:53:41.789
The return shows up as feature lead time: §1.1's five-service features became one-context features, which is where the re-architecture paid for itself.

610
00:53:43.139 --> 00:53:44.078
Part 7.

611
00:53:44.358 --> 00:53:46.065
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

612
00:53:47.315 --> 00:53:48.232
Watch out:

613
00:53:48.512 --> 00:53:49.152
1.

614
00:53:49.432 --> 00:53:51.181
Splitting services by nouns.

615
00:53:51.461 --> 00:53:57.349
One service per entity or table puts every real use case across many services (L4:

616
00:53:57.629 --> 00:54:00.659
79% of weekly work crossed boundaries).

617
00:54:00.939 --> 00:54:02.645
Split by bounded context.

618
00:54:03.995 --> 00:54:04.913
Watch out:

619
00:54:05.193 --> 00:54:05.897
2.

620
00:54:06.177 --> 00:54:07.691
The anemic domain model.

621
00:54:07.971 --> 00:54:14.435
Getters or setters plus services full of rules; every new code path is a chance to skip a rule (L1).

622
00:54:14.715 --> 00:54:17.467
Put invariants in aggregates and value objects.

623
00:54:18.817 --> 00:54:19.735
Watch out:

624
00:54:20.015 --> 00:54:20.761
3.

625
00:54:21.041 --> 00:54:22.321
The god aggregate.

626
00:54:22.601 --> 00:54:25.055
StoreInventory, Customer-with-all-orders.

627
00:54:25.335 --> 00:54:29.239
Big aggregates serialize writes and load huge graphs (L2).

628
00:54:29.519 --> 00:54:31.097
Size to the true invariant.

629
00:54:32.447 --> 00:54:33.365
Watch out:

630
00:54:33.645 --> 00:54:34.349
4.

631
00:54:34.629 --> 00:54:37.615
Modifying several aggregates in one transaction.

632
00:54:37.895 --> 00:54:41.565
Couples their lifecycles and locks, and fails across services.

633
00:54:41.845 --> 00:54:45.791
One aggregate per transaction; events or sagas for the rest.

634
00:54:47.141 --> 00:54:48.059
Watch out:

635
00:54:48.339 --> 00:54:49.107
5.

636
00:54:49.387 --> 00:54:51.221
The enterprise canonical model.

637
00:54:51.501 --> 00:54:56.963
One Product for the whole company becomes a 63-column table nobody can change.

638
00:54:57.243 --> 00:55:00.443
Each context gets its own model; share identifiers.

639
00:55:01.793 --> 00:55:02.710
Watch out:

640
00:55:02.990 --> 00:55:03.737
6.

641
00:55:04.017 --> 00:55:05.958
Integrating without an ACL.

642
00:55:06.238 --> 00:55:12.297
Letting a legacy or third-party model leak in (L3) spreads its concepts and bugs through your domain.

643
00:55:12.577 --> 00:55:14.027
Translate at the boundary.

644
00:55:15.377 --> 00:55:16.295
Watch out:

645
00:55:16.575 --> 00:55:17.321
7.

646
00:55:17.601 --> 00:55:20.503
Tactical DDD without strategic DDD.

647
00:55:20.783 --> 00:55:27.396
Classes named...Aggregate and...Repository inside wrong boundaries give you ceremony without benefit.

648
00:55:27.676 --> 00:55:29.276
Do the boundary work first.

649
00:55:30.626 --> 00:55:31.543
Watch out:

650
00:55:31.823 --> 00:55:32.506
8.

651
00:55:32.786 --> 00:55:34.194
DDD everywhere.

652
00:55:34.474 --> 00:55:36.693
Rich aggregates for a CRUD settings page.

653
00:55:36.973 --> 00:55:42.477
Use simple code for simple subdomains; buy generic ones (§2.11).

654
00:55:43.827 --> 00:55:44.744
Watch out:

655
00:55:45.024 --> 00:55:45.771
9.

656
00:55:46.051 --> 00:55:47.245
Primitive obsession.

657
00:55:47.525 --> 00:55:50.021
String pincode, double price, int status.

658
00:55:50.301 --> 00:55:52.733
Every consumer re-validates (or doesn't).

659
00:55:53.013 --> 00:55:59.797
Use value objects (L3's 78.85 rupees, then 78.84 rupees).

660
00:56:01.147 --> 00:56:02.065
Watch out:

661
00:56:02.345 --> 00:56:03.049
10.

662
00:56:03.329 --> 00:56:05.483
Querying another context's database.

663
00:56:05.763 --> 00:56:09.027
Couples you to its schema and bypasses its invariants.

664
00:56:09.307 --> 00:56:12.102
Use its API, its events, or a read model.

665
00:56:13.452 --> 00:56:14.369
Watch out:

666
00:56:14.649 --> 00:56:15.481
11.

667
00:56:15.761 --> 00:56:26.321
Business logic in application services. if (order.getStatus() == PACKED and and user.isAdmin()) in a handler means the rule isn't in the domain.

668
00:56:26.601 --> 00:56:28.180
Move it into the aggregate.

669
00:56:29.530 --> 00:56:30.447
Watch out:

670
00:56:30.727 --> 00:56:31.517
12.

671
00:56:31.797 --> 00:56:33.994
Letting an algorithm draw your boundaries.

672
00:56:34.274 --> 00:56:42.743
Clustering is evidence, not an answer, L4's modularity result grouped Cart, Coupon and StockLevel by call pattern alone.

673
00:56:43.023 --> 00:56:45.690
Decide on language, invariants and ownership.

674
00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:47.915
Part 8.

675
00:56:48.195 --> 00:56:49.539
Interview Q and A.

676
00:56:50.789 --> 00:56:51.621
Beginner.

677
00:56:51.901 --> 00:56:52.925
Question 1.

678
00:56:53.205 --> 00:56:54.783
What is a bounded context?

679
00:56:55.063 --> 00:56:56.429
Thirty-second answer:

680
00:56:56.709 --> 00:57:04.410
A boundary within which a particular domain model and its language are consistent, inside it, each term means exactly one thing.

681
00:57:04.690 --> 00:57:14.055
Product in Catalog is a listing with images; in Inventory it's stock of a SKU in a dark store; in Fulfilment it's a physical item with a weight.

682
00:57:14.335 --> 00:57:19.669
Each context has its own model and data, and they integrate through explicit relationships.

683
00:57:21.019 --> 00:57:22.021
Question 2.

684
00:57:22.301 --> 00:57:24.264
Entity versus value object?

685
00:57:24.544 --> 00:57:25.909
Thirty-second answer:

686
00:57:26.189 --> 00:57:34.445
An entity has an identity that persists through changes, an Order is the same order from draft to delivered; equality is by ID.

687
00:57:34.725 --> 00:57:48.336
A value object is defined only by its attributes, is immutable and has no identity, Money(2850 paise), Pincode(560034); equality is by value.

688
00:57:48.616 --> 00:57:53.288
Value objects are ideal for validating values once and carrying behavior.

689
00:57:54.638 --> 00:57:55.683
Question 3.

690
00:57:55.963 --> 00:57:57.563
What is ubiquitous language?

691
00:57:57.843 --> 00:57:59.209
Thirty-second answer:

692
00:57:59.489 --> 00:58:07.041
A shared vocabulary used by domain experts and developers everywhere, in conversations, docs and the code itself.

693
00:58:07.321 --> 00:58:10.841
If operations says short-pick, the code has a ShortPick event.

694
00:58:11.121 --> 00:58:15.302
It removes translation errors and makes code reviewable by the business.

695
00:58:15.582 --> 00:58:20.019
It's bounded: consistent within a context, not across the whole enterprise.

696
00:58:21.369 --> 00:58:22.436
Question 4.

697
00:58:22.716 --> 00:58:26.257
Core, supporting and generic subdomains, why classify?

698
00:58:26.537 --> 00:58:27.903
Thirty-second answer:

699
00:58:28.183 --> 00:58:29.655
It's an investment decision.

700
00:58:29.935 --> 00:58:35.695
Core subdomains differentiate the business, build them in-house with your best people and rich modeling.

701
00:58:35.975 --> 00:58:41.457
Supporting subdomains are necessary and specific but not differentiating, build them simply.

702
00:58:41.737 --> 00:58:48.820
Generic subdomains are solved problems (payments, SMS, identity), buy or use off-the-shelf and integrate.

703
00:58:50.170 --> 00:58:51.173
Intermediate.

704
00:58:51.453 --> 00:58:52.562
Question 5.

705
00:58:52.842 --> 00:58:56.063
What is an aggregate and what are the rules for designing one?

706
00:58:56.343 --> 00:58:57.709
Thirty-second answer:

707
00:58:57.989 --> 00:59:06.714
A cluster of entities and value objects changed as one unit, with a root that is the only entry point, whose job is to enforce invariants.

708
00:59:06.994 --> 00:59:24.018
Rules: protect true invariants inside the boundary through intention-revealing methods; keep aggregates small; reference other aggregates by ID; modify one aggregate per transaction and use eventual consistency (events, sagas) between aggregates.

709
00:59:25.368 --> 00:59:26.477
Question 6.

710
00:59:26.757 --> 00:59:29.723
What is an anti-corruption layer and when do you need one?

711
00:59:30.003 --> 00:59:31.368
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:59:31.648 --> 00:59:39.605
A translation layer between your bounded context and an external or legacy model, so the external concepts don't leak into your domain.

713
00:59:39.885 --> 00:59:46.968
It converts their data into your ubiquitous language, validates it, and rejects or quarantines what it can't translate.

714
00:59:47.248 --> 00:59:56.592
You need one whenever you integrate with a legacy system, a third party whose model you don't control, or another context whose model differs significantly.

715
00:59:57.942 --> 00:59:59.030
Question 7.

716
00:59:59.310 --> 01:00:02.617
What are domain events and how are they used across contexts?

717
01:00:02.897 --> 01:00:04.262
Thirty-second answer:

718
01:00:04.542 --> 01:00:10.921
Records of something meaningful that happened in the domain, named in the past tense, OrderPlaced, StockReserved.

719
01:00:11.201 --> 01:00:20.502
Within a context they decouple side effects; across contexts they're the main integration mechanism, giving eventual consistency between aggregates.

720
01:00:20.782 --> 01:00:27.651
Publish them reliably with a transactional outbox, and treat cross-context events as a versioned published language.

721
01:00:29.001 --> 01:00:30.025
Question 8.

722
01:00:30.305 --> 01:00:32.097
Repository versus DAO?

723
01:00:32.377 --> 01:00:33.743
Thirty-second answer:

724
01:00:34.023 --> 01:00:36.903
A DAO is table-oriented, CRUD per table.

725
01:00:37.183 --> 01:00:47.615
A repository is aggregate-oriented, a collection-like interface for loading and saving whole aggregates, defined in the domain and implemented in infrastructure.

726
01:00:47.895 --> 01:00:55.020
There's one repository per aggregate root, and it never exposes partial aggregates or lets callers bypass the root.

727
01:00:56.370 --> 01:00:57.437
Question 9.

728
01:00:57.717 --> 01:01:00.405
Domain service versus application service?

729
01:01:00.685 --> 01:01:02.050
Thirty-second answer:

730
01:01:02.330 --> 01:01:12.975
A domain service holds domain logic that doesn't belong to one entity, often a policy across aggregates, named in the ubiquitous language (SlotAllocationService).

731
01:01:13.255 --> 01:01:23.751
An application service orchestrates a use case, load aggregates, call domain methods, save, publish, handle transactions and auth, and contains no business rules.

732
01:01:25.101 --> 01:01:25.869
Senior.

733
01:01:26.149 --> 01:01:27.195
Question 10.

734
01:01:27.475 --> 01:01:30.525
How do you find bounded contexts in an existing system?

735
01:01:30.805 --> 01:01:32.171
Thirty-second answer:

736
01:01:32.451 --> 01:01:50.691
Combine signals: run event storming with domain experts to find where language changes and where flows hand off; identify invariants that must be consistent together; look at change coupling in git history; analyze which entities use cases touch together; and consider team ownership.

737
01:01:50.971 --> 01:01:54.917
Draft contexts with experts, then use data to challenge the draft.

738
01:01:56.267 --> 01:01:57.398
Question 11.

739
01:01:57.678 --> 01:01:59.385
How do you size an aggregate?

740
01:01:59.665 --> 01:02:01.030
Thirty-second answer:

741
01:02:01.310 --> 01:02:03.294
As small as the true invariants allow.

742
01:02:03.574 --> 01:02:10.166
Ask which data must be consistent immediately, in one transaction, for a rule to hold, that's the aggregate.

743
01:02:10.446 --> 01:02:14.627
Everything else is referenced by ID and made eventually consistent.

744
01:02:14.907 --> 01:02:21.606
Then check concurrency: the aggregate is the unit of locking, so its write contention sets your throughput ceiling.

745
01:02:22.956 --> 01:02:24.044
Question 12.

746
01:02:24.324 --> 01:02:28.271
Explain the context-map relationship patterns and when to use each.

747
01:02:28.551 --> 01:02:29.916
Thirty-second answer:

748
01:02:30.196 --> 01:02:47.924
Partnership (tightly coordinated teams), shared kernel (small jointly-owned model, keep tiny), customer or supplier (downstream needs shape upstream priorities), conformist (adopt upstream's model, when it's good and you have no leverage), ACL (translate to protect your model),

749
01:02:48.204 --> 01:02:59.041
open host service (upstream offers a stable protocol for many consumers), published language (documented shared format), separate ways (duplicate instead of integrate).

750
01:03:00.391 --> 01:03:01.671
Question 13.

751
01:03:01.951 --> 01:03:05.685
How do you keep an invariant that spans two aggregates or contexts?

752
01:03:05.965 --> 01:03:07.330
Thirty-second answer:

753
01:03:07.610 --> 01:03:14.031
First, check whether it truly must be immediate, often the business accepts a short delay plus compensation.

754
01:03:14.311 --> 01:03:23.719
If eventual is fine: emit a domain event from the first aggregate and let the second react, or coordinate with a saga that includes compensation.

755
01:03:23.999 --> 01:03:29.589
If it must be immediate, the two pieces belong in one aggregate, and you accept the contention.

756
01:03:30.939 --> 01:03:32.133
Question 14.

757
01:03:32.413 --> 01:03:34.675
When should you not use tactical DDD?

758
01:03:34.955 --> 01:03:36.320
Thirty-second answer:

759
01:03:36.600 --> 01:03:42.744
For CRUD, generic subdomains, reporting, and simple data-centric features where rules are trivial.

760
01:03:43.024 --> 01:03:52.240
Tactical DDD adds classes, mapping and eventual consistency; it pays off only with complex, evolving rules and concurrent actors.

761
01:03:52.520 --> 01:03:58.899
Strategic DDD, language, bounded contexts, context maps, ACLs, is almost always worth it.

762
01:04:00.249 --> 01:04:01.145
Architect.

763
01:04:01.425 --> 01:04:02.619
Question 15.

764
01:04:02.899 --> 01:04:06.611
How do bounded contexts map to microservices and teams?

765
01:04:06.891 --> 01:04:08.257
Thirty-second answer:

766
01:04:08.537 --> 01:04:15.491
A bounded context is the natural unit of ownership: one team per context (a team can own several small ones).

767
01:04:15.771 --> 01:04:17.862
Services should not straddle contexts.

768
01:04:18.142 --> 01:04:30.558
Start with contexts as modules in a modular monolith and extract a context into its own service when there's a real driver, independent scaling, release cadence, availability or team boundary.

769
01:04:30.838 --> 01:04:35.531
A large context may be several services, but they share its language and team.

770
01:04:36.881 --> 01:04:38.076
Question 16.

771
01:04:38.356 --> 01:04:41.983
How would you migrate from a shared database to context-owned data?

772
01:04:42.263 --> 01:04:43.628
Thirty-second answer:

773
01:04:43.908 --> 01:05:05.284
Incrementally with the strangler pattern: identify which context owns each table (split tables whose columns belong to different contexts), give each context its own schema, route writes through the owner's API, replace cross-context queries with APIs, events or read models, and use CDC or outbox for data others need.

774
01:05:05.564 --> 01:05:10.983
Move one context at a time behind an ACL, with expand or contract migrations.

775
01:05:12.333 --> 01:05:13.677
Question 17.

776
01:05:13.957 --> 01:05:17.541
How do DDD and event sourcing or CQRS relate?

777
01:05:17.821 --> 01:05:19.186
Thirty-second answer:

778
01:05:19.466 --> 01:05:21.685
They're complementary but independent.

779
01:05:21.965 --> 01:05:35.810
DDD gives you aggregates that emit domain events; event sourcing stores the aggregate as that stream of events instead of current state; CQRS separates the write model (aggregates) from read models (projections).

780
01:05:36.090 --> 01:05:45.541
Use event sourcing where history is itself valuable (ledgers, audit-heavy domains); use CQRS where reads span contexts or need different shapes.

781
01:05:45.821 --> 01:05:47.890
Many DDD systems use neither.

782
01:05:49.240 --> 01:05:50.413
Question 18.

783
01:05:50.693 --> 01:05:54.576
How do you run an event-storming workshop and turn it into architecture?

784
01:05:54.856 --> 01:05:56.221
Thirty-second answer:

785
01:05:56.501 --> 01:06:00.235
Get domain experts and engineers in a room (or a virtual board).

786
01:06:00.515 --> 01:06:09.176
Big-picture pass: everyone writes domain events on a timeline; then add hotspots, actors, external systems, commands and policies.

787
01:06:09.456 --> 01:06:12.976
Find boundaries where language changes and flows hand off.

788
01:06:13.256 --> 01:06:19.528
Then a design-level pass per candidate context: commands, aggregates, invariants, read models.

789
01:06:19.808 --> 01:06:29.259
Output: a context map, aggregate candidates, events as the published language, and hotspots as risks, captured in ADRs and a design doc.

790
01:06:30.609 --> 01:06:31.846
Question 19.

791
01:06:32.126 --> 01:06:36.499
Your team has 'DDD' but every change still touches five services.

792
01:06:36.779 --> 01:06:37.825
Diagnose it.

793
01:06:38.105 --> 01:06:39.470
Thirty-second answer:

794
01:06:39.750 --> 01:06:50.566
Likely tactical DDD without strategic DDD: services drawn by entity, shared models, cross-service transactions, business rules in application services.

795
01:06:50.846 --> 01:07:02.259
Measure change coupling (features spanning contexts), look for shared tables and cross-service synchronous chains, and check whether each service maps to a coherent language and invariant set.

796
01:07:02.539 --> 01:07:06.571
Then re-draw boundaries via event storming and migrate incrementally.

797
01:07:07.921 --> 01:07:09.031
Question 20.

798
01:07:09.311 --> 01:07:11.764
How do you decide what goes into a shared kernel?

799
01:07:12.044 --> 01:07:13.409
Thirty-second answer:

800
01:07:13.689 --> 01:07:14.735
Almost nothing.

801
01:07:15.015 --> 01:07:30.204
Only small, stable, genuinely identical concepts that several contexts must agree on exactly, typically value objects like Money, Quantity or Unit, Pincode, SkuCode, versioned as a library and changed only by agreement.

802
01:07:30.484 --> 01:07:35.049
Anything with context-specific meaning (Product, Customer, Order) stays out.

803
01:07:36.399 --> 01:07:37.295
Part 9.

804
01:07:37.575 --> 01:07:38.983
Summary and Practice.

805
01:07:40.233 --> 01:07:41.321
Key takeaways.

806
01:07:41.601 --> 01:07:44.503
DDD makes software boundaries follow the business.

807
01:07:44.783 --> 01:07:52.271
Strategic DDD (contexts, language, maps) delivers most of the architectural value (§2.1).

808
01:07:52.551 --> 01:08:00.508
Classify subdomains and invest accordingly: build the core richly, the supporting simply, buy the generic (§2.2).

809
01:08:00.788 --> 01:08:08.553
Ubiquitous language is bounded, where meanings change, you've found a context boundary (§2.3, §2.4).

810
01:08:08.833 --> 01:08:17.281
Find contexts from language, invariants, change and usage data; use data to challenge expert drafts, not replace them (L4).

811
01:08:18.631 --> 01:08:24.455
Make relationships explicit on a context map, and protect your model with an ACL (L3).

812
01:08:24.735 --> 01:08:30.474
Aggregates own invariants, no setters, intention-revealing methods, derived totals (L1).

813
01:08:30.754 --> 01:08:36.727
Size aggregates to the true invariant, the aggregate is also the unit of concurrency (L2).

814
01:08:37.007 --> 01:08:46.522
One aggregate per transaction; events and sagas between them, published reliably via outbox (§2.7, §2.9).

815
01:08:47.872 --> 01:08:54.187
Contexts map to teams; modules first, services when there's a driver (§2.10).

816
01:08:55.537 --> 01:08:56.859
Decision cheat sheet.

817
01:08:57.139 --> 01:08:58.483
Here is a quick table:

818
01:08:58.763 --> 01:09:00.086
Question, and Answer.

819
01:09:00.366 --> 01:09:02.606
Where are the service or module boundaries?:

820
01:09:02.886 --> 01:09:06.982
Bounded contexts, found by language, invariants, usage, ownership.

821
01:09:07.262 --> 01:09:08.905
Same word, different meanings?:

822
01:09:09.185 --> 01:09:11.979
Separate contexts, separate models, shared ID.

823
01:09:12.259 --> 01:09:14.798
Integrating a legacy or third-party model?:

824
01:09:15.078 --> 01:09:18.043
ACL (or conformist if it's good and stable).

825
01:09:18.323 --> 01:09:19.966
Where does a business rule live?:

826
01:09:20.246 --> 01:09:23.254
In the aggregate or value object that owns the data.

827
01:09:23.534 --> 01:09:25.369
How big should an aggregate be?:

828
01:09:25.649 --> 01:09:28.401
As small as the invariant; check conflict rates.

829
01:09:28.681 --> 01:09:30.323
Rule across two aggregates?:

830
01:09:30.603 --> 01:09:35.617
Event + eventual consistency, or saga; merge only if truly immediate.

831
01:09:35.897 --> 01:09:37.305
Money, units, codes?:

832
01:09:37.585 --> 01:09:39.654
Value objects, never primitives.

833
01:09:41.004 --> 01:09:42.711
CRUD or generic subdomain?:

834
01:09:42.991 --> 01:09:46.489
Simple code or a bought product, not tactical DDD.

835
01:09:46.769 --> 01:09:48.497
What to share between contexts?:

836
01:09:48.777 --> 01:09:51.977
IDs, events, a tiny kernel of value objects.

837
01:09:53.327 --> 01:09:54.458
Exercises.

838
01:09:54.738 --> 01:10:01.650
(B2) Add a packed() transition to L1's aggregate and a rule that items can't be removed after packing.

839
01:10:01.930 --> 01:10:07.391
Add the support-tool scenario from §1.1 and show the anemic model accepting it.

840
01:10:07.671 --> 01:10:14.071
(B3) Extend L2 with a fourth design: per dark store and per SKU, across 20 dark stores.

841
01:10:14.351 --> 01:10:16.143
How does the conflict rate change?

842
01:10:16.423 --> 01:10:19.431
What does that say about sharding aggregates by location?

843
01:10:19.711 --> 01:10:34.026
(B3) Extend L3's ACL to parse pack size from the description (500ML, 1KG, 12PC) into a Quantity value object, quarantining rows where description and UOM disagree.

844
01:10:35.376 --> 01:10:43.568
(B4) Replace L4's usage data with change coupling from a real repo's git history (files changed together in commits).

845
01:10:43.848 --> 01:10:46.515
Do the contexts it finds match your team boundaries?

846
01:10:46.795 --> 01:10:52.811
(B4) Draw your own system's context map with the §2.5 relationship patterns.

847
01:10:53.091 --> 01:10:55.544
Where is a conformist relationship hurting you?

848
01:10:55.824 --> 01:10:57.339
Where would an ACL help?

849
01:10:58.689 --> 01:10:59.691
Further reading.

850
01:10:59.971 --> 01:11:02.233
Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design:

851
01:11:02.513 --> 01:11:05.926
Tackling Complexity in the Heart of Software (2003).

852
01:11:06.206 --> 01:11:14.974
Vaughn Vernon, Implementing Domain-Driven Design and Domain-Driven Design Distilled; Effective Aggregate Design (three-part essay).

853
01:11:15.254 --> 01:11:21.377
Vlad Khononov, Learning Domain-Driven Design, subdomains, context mapping, practical tactics.

854
01:11:21.657 --> 01:11:24.579
Alberto Brandolini, Introducing EventStorming.

855
01:11:24.859 --> 01:11:31.110
Nick Tune and Jean-Georges Perrin, Architecture Modernization, contexts, teams and migration.

856
01:11:32.460 --> 01:11:39.031
DDD Crew (github.com or ddd-crew), bounded context canvas, context mapping cheat sheets.

857
01:11:40.381 --> 01:11:41.618
